  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThe ridge of high pressure will start to move back to the west \nslightly tonight into Tuesday. This will leave the area more in \nthe upper level northwest flow. A weak front is expected to move\nsouth towards the area as an upper level disturbance moves \nthrough the northwest flow on Tuesday into Tuesday night. \nShowers and thunderstorm development will be possible across \nthe central Plains this evening into tonight ahead of the upper \nlevel disturbance. There will be the potential for this activity\nto move southeast into the area Tuesday. The activity will\nlikely be weakening as it moves towards the area, if it can make\nit into the area. Some uncapped elevated instability may \nremain in place across the area on Tuesday therefore there will\nbe the potential for a few showers and thunderstorms to be\npossible at times on Tuesday, and with peak heating some \nscattered afternoon/evening storms redeveloping will be possible\ntoo.\n\nThe hot air mass will remain in place across the area again on\nTuesday, but with the potential for increased clouds and some \nrain potential for some of the area, temperatures could be \ncooler on Tuesday. However, if the rain does not develop and if \nclouds dissipate during the afternoon hours, the hotter \nconditions will occur again on Tuesday with highs in the middle \n90s to near 100 degrees. Less mixing may occur on Tuesday, so it\nis possible for heat index values to warm to around 110 degrees\nagain Tuesday afternoon/early evening where plenty of sunshine \noccurs. But if more clouds occur through the afternoon/early\nevening hours temperatures and heat index values will be \ncooler. Will continue the Heat Advisory across the area on \nTuesday based on the uncertainty on rain and cloud coverage \npotential. It is possible portions of the area could approach \nExtreme Heat Warning levels again on Tuesday if less clouds \noccur south of the front Tuesday. \n\nThe ridge pushes back to the west more during the middle and end\nof the week. The hotter air mass will also push back to the \nwest of the area with temperatures returning closer to normal, \nin the upper 80s to lower/mid 90s mid and late week. The ridge \nwill push further to the east this weekend with highs in the \n80s expected. \n\nThe area will remain in a northwest flow pattern Thursday night\ninto Saturday. An upper level disturbance will move through the\nthe region in the northwest flow and will bring shower and\nthunderstorm chances (30-60%) to the area Thursday night into \nFriday and could linger into Saturday. The exact track and how \nwidespread the rain will be is still questions as it is possible\nit is more of a band of scattered showers and storms. If \nshowers and storms can train over the same locations there would\nbe the potential for locally heavy rainfall Thursday night into\nFriday across portions of the area.",
